## Phase 0 — Prerequisites & Feasibility

### Can You Do This on One VM?

**Yes**, but with specific constraints:

| Concern | Solution |
|---|---|
| UPF needs `NET_ADMIN` + TUN device | Pods run as **privileged** with `NET_ADMIN` capability |
| UPF must reach SMF on `127.0.0.x` (PFCP) | Use `hostNetwork: true` — pod shares the host's network stack |
| GTP-U must be reachable from gNB | `hostNetwork: true` exposes GTP-U on host IPs directly |
| `tc` must be available inside container | Install `iproute2` in the Docker image |
| TUN device (`ogstun-xxx`) creation | UPF creates it; needs [/dev/net/tun](file:///dev/net/tun) device mounted |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Using `hostNetwork: true` is **critical**. Without it, UPF pods would be on the minikube bridge network (192.168.58.x) and could not reach `127.0.0.x` loopback addresses where your SMFs listen for PFCP. It also means Kubernetes Services/ClusterIPs won't be used for UPF traffic — the pod directly shares the host's network interfaces.

### What You Must Stop First

Before deploying UPF pods, **stop the native UPF processes** to free the ports:

```bash
# Stop native UPFs (they will be replaced by pods)
sudo systemctl stop open5gs-upfd      # default UPF
# If using custom systemd units for slice UPFs:
sudo systemctl stop open5gs-upfd-embb
sudo systemctl stop open5gs-upfd-urllc
sudo systemctl stop open5gs-upfd-mmtc
```

> [!CAUTION]
> The native SMFs (smf-embb, smf-urllc, smf-mmtc) stay **running as-is**. Only the UPFs move into Kubernetes. **Do NOT stop the SMFs.**

---

## Phase 1 — Build the UPF Docker Image

### Dockerfile

Create a single Dockerfile that can run any of the 3 UPFs based on a mounted config:

```dockerfile
FROM ubuntu:22.04

RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y \
    software-properties-common \
    && add-apt-repository ppa:open5gs/latest \
    && apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y \
      open5gs-upf \
      iproute2 \
      iptables \
      iputils-ping \
      net-tools \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# Entrypoint script handles TUN creation, tc setup, then launches UPF
COPY entrypoint.sh /entrypoint.sh
RUN chmod +x /entrypoint.sh

ENTRYPOINT ["/entrypoint.sh"]
```

### Entrypoint Script (`entrypoint.sh`)

```bash
#!/bin/bash
set -e

# --- Environment variables (set from K8s manifests) ---
TUN_DEV=${TUN_DEV:-ogstun}
UE_SUBNET=${UE_SUBNET:-10.45.0.0/24}
UE_GATEWAY=${UE_GATEWAY:-10.45.0.1}
UPF_CONFIG=${UPF_CONFIG:-/etc/open5gs/upf.yaml}

# Slice-specific tc shaping (env vars from deployment)
TC_RATE=${TC_RATE:-}          # e.g., "100mbit"
TC_CEIL=${TC_CEIL:-}          # e.g., "100mbit"
TC_LATENCY=${TC_LATENCY:-}   # e.g., "1ms"  (for netem)
TC_PRIORITY=${TC_PRIORITY:-}  # e.g., "1" (for prio qdisc)

# --- 1. Create TUN device ---
echo "[entrypoint] Creating TUN device: $TUN_DEV"
ip tuntap add name $TUN_DEV mode tun
ip addr add $UE_GATEWAY dev $TUN_DEV
ip link set $TUN_DEV up

# --- 2. Enable IP forwarding + NAT ---
echo "[entrypoint] Setting up IP forwarding and NAT"
sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1
iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s $UE_SUBNET ! -o $TUN_DEV -j MASQUERADE

# --- 3. Apply tc shaping (if configured) ---
if [ -n "$TC_RATE" ]; then
    echo "[entrypoint] Applying tc shaping: rate=$TC_RATE ceil=$TC_CEIL"
    tc qdisc add dev $TUN_DEV root handle 1: htb default 1
    tc class add dev $TUN_DEV parent 1: classid 1:1 htb \
        rate $TC_RATE ceil ${TC_CEIL:-$TC_RATE}

    # Add netem for latency (URLLC gets ultra-low, mMTC gets tolerance)
    if [ -n "$TC_LATENCY" ]; then
        echo "[entrypoint] Adding netem: latency=$TC_LATENCY"
        tc qdisc add dev $TUN_DEV parent 1:1 handle 10: netem \
            delay $TC_LATENCY
    fi
fi

# --- 4. Launch UPF ---
echo "[entrypoint] Starting open5gs-upfd with config: $UPF_CONFIG"
exec open5gs-upfd -c $UPF_CONFIG
```

### Build & Load into Minikube

```bash
# Point docker CLI to minikube's daemon (avoids needing a registry)
eval $(minikube docker-env)

# Build inside minikube's docker
docker build -t open5gs-upf:local -f Dockerfile .

# Verify
docker images | grep open5gs-upf
```

---

## Phase 2 — Kubernetes Manifests (Per-Slice)

### Strategy: One UPF Deployment Per Namespace

Each slice gets:
- A **ConfigMap** with the UPF YAML (modified for container paths)
- A **Deployment** (1 replica, privileged, hostNetwork)
- A **ClusterIP Service** (for monitoring/metrics, not for PFCP traffic which goes via host)

### 2a. eMBB UPF (namespace: `embb`)

#### ConfigMap

The UPF config is almost identical to your current [upf-embb.yaml](file:///etc/open5gs/upf-embb.yaml), but the `session.dev` field is removed (the entrypoint creates the TUN device):

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: upf-embb-config
  namespace: embb
data:
  upf-embb.yaml: |
    logger:
      file:
        path: /var/log/open5gs/upf-embb.log
    global:
      max:
        ue: 1024
    upf:
      pfcp:
        server:
          - address: 127.0.0.7
            port: 8805
      gtpu:
        server:
          - address: 192.168.49.143
            port: 2152
      session:
        - subnet: 10.45.0.0/24
          gateway: 10.45.0.1
          dnn: internet
```

#### Deployment

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: upf-embb
  namespace: embb
  labels:
    app: upf-embb
    slice: embb
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: upf-embb
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: upf-embb
        slice: embb
    spec:
      hostNetwork: true          # Share host's network (reach 127.0.0.x)
      dnsPolicy: ClusterFirstWithHostNet
      containers:
        - name: upf
          image: open5gs-upf:local
          imagePullPolicy: Never  # Use locally built image
          securityContext:
            privileged: true      # Needed for TUN, tc, iptables
            capabilities:
              add: ["NET_ADMIN", "SYS_ADMIN"]
          env:
            - name: TUN_DEV
              value: "ogstun-embb"
            - name: UE_SUBNET
              value: "10.45.0.0/24"
            - name: UE_GATEWAY
              value: "10.45.0.1/24"
            - name: UPF_CONFIG
              value: "/config/upf-embb.yaml"
            # tc shaping: eMBB = high bandwidth
            - name: TC_RATE
              value: "100mbit"
            - name: TC_CEIL
              value: "100mbit"
          volumeMounts:
            - name: config
              mountPath: /config
            - name: dev-net-tun
              mountPath: /dev/net/tun
          resources:
            requests:
              memory: "128Mi"
              cpu: "100m"
            limits:
              memory: "512Mi"
              cpu: "500m"
      volumes:
        - name: config
          configMap:
            name: upf-embb-config
        - name: dev-net-tun
          hostPath:
            path: /dev/net/tun
            type: CharDevice
```

#### Service (metrics endpoint)

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: upf-embb-metrics
  namespace: embb
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app: upf-embb
  ports:
    - name: metrics
      port: 9090
      targetPort: 9090
```

### 2b. URLLC UPF (namespace: `urllc`)

Same pattern, different values:

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| PFCP | `127.0.0.8:8806` |
| GTP-U | `192.168.49.144:2152` |
| Subnet | `10.46.0.0/24` |
| Gateway | `10.46.0.1/24` |
| TUN device | `ogstun-urllc` |
| DNN | `urllc` |
| tc rate | `50mbit` (bandwidth-constrained, latency-optimized) |
| tc latency | `1ms` (ultra-low via netem) |

### 2c. mMTC UPF (namespace: `mmtc`)

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| PFCP | `127.0.0.9:8807` |
| GTP-U | `192.168.49.146:2152` |
| Subnet | `10.47.0.0/24` |
| Gateway | `10.47.0.1/24` |
| TUN device | `ogstun-mmtc` |
| DNN | `iot` |
| tc rate | `1mbit` (low bandwidth for IoT sensors) |
| tc ceil | `5mbit` |

---

## Phase 3 — SMF Config Changes (Minimal)

> [!NOTE]
> Because we use `hostNetwork: true`, the UPF pods will bind to the **exact same addresses** as your current native UPFs (`127.0.0.7:8805`, `127.0.0.8:8806`, `127.0.0.9:8807`). **No changes to the SMF configs are required.** The SMFs won't even know the UPFs moved to Kubernetes.

The only change needed is **stopping the native UPF processes** so the ports are free for the pods.

---

## Phase 4 — Deployment Sequence

```mermaid
graph LR
    A["1. Build Docker<br/>image"] --> B["2. Stop native<br/>UPFs"]
    B --> C["3. Deploy eMBB<br/>UPF pod"]
    C --> D["4. Deploy URLLC<br/>UPF pod"]
    D --> E["5. Deploy mMTC<br/>UPF pod"]
    E --> F["6. Verify PFCP<br/>association"]
    F --> G["7. Test PDU<br/>session + ping"]
    G --> H["8. Verify tc<br/>shaping"]
```

### Step-by-Step Commands

```bash
# 1. Build image (inside minikube docker)
eval $(minikube docker-env)
docker build -t open5gs-upf:local .

# 2. Stop native UPFs
sudo systemctl stop open5gs-upfd-embb
sudo systemctl stop open5gs-upfd-urllc
sudo systemctl stop open5gs-upfd-mmtc

# 3-5. Deploy
kubectl apply -f k8s/embb/configmap.yaml
kubectl apply -f k8s/embb/deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f k8s/urllc/configmap.yaml
kubectl apply -f k8s/urllc/deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f k8s/mmtc/configmap.yaml
kubectl apply -f k8s/mmtc/deployment.yaml

# 6. Verify pods are running
kubectl get pods -n embb -l app=upf-embb
kubectl get pods -n urllc -l app=upf-urllc
kubectl get pods -n mmtc -l app=upf-mmtc

# 7. Check PFCP association in SMF logs
tail -f /var/log/open5gs/smf-embb.log | grep -i pfcp
tail -f /var/log/open5gs/smf-urllc.log | grep -i pfcp
tail -f /var/log/open5gs/smf-mmtc.log | grep -i pfcp

# 8. Test from UERANSIM
sudo ./nr-ue -c ue-embb.yaml
ping 10.45.0.1  # via ogstun-embb (now inside pod)
```

